-- title:
--   Primes other than q are units in a DVR with residue field of order q
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ and $q'$ be primes, and let $\mathcal{O}$ be a commutative ring which is a domain, assumed to be a discrete valuation ring (this assumption is supplied as an explicit hypothesis rather than as an instance). Let $\pi \in \mathcal{O}$ be irreducible, and suppose the quotient $\mathcal{O}/(\pi)$ by the ideal generated by $\pi$ has cardinality exactly $q$ in the sense of `Nat.card`. Let $\ell$ be an element of `HeckeTower.AwayPrime q q'`, that is, a prime number $\ell$ (an element of `Nat.Primes`) together with proofs that $\ell \neq q$ and $\ell \neq q'$. The conclusion is that the image of the natural number $\ell$ under the canonical map $\mathbb{N} \to \mathcal{O}$ is a unit of $\mathcal{O}$.
--
--   A routine local-algebra fact, stated over the index type of the Hecke tower away from $\{q,q'\}$ so that it can be applied directly: in a discrete valuation ring whose residue field has $q$ elements, any prime $\ell \neq q$ is invertible. It is used in the Čerednik–Drinfel'd tower constructions, where Hecke operators and level structures away from $q$ and $q'$ require $\ell$ to be invertible in the coefficient ring.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_CerednikDrinfeld_HeckeTower_AwayPrime_isUnit_natCast_of_card_residue.lean

